  <section id="intro">
    <h2>1 · Introduction and Revision Notice</h2>
    <p>GUES v0.1 proposed that spacetime geometry emerges from information flow, that the fundamental forces are symmetry structures within that emergent geometry, and that black holes function as information-to-geometry transducers driving cosmic expansion. That paper was labeled E1 and invited adversarial review.</p>
    <p>The review arrived. It was good. This is the revision it produced.</p>
    <p>The central correction: information flow is not the primitive substrate. Information requires distinguishable states, admissible transitions, and preserved differences. Those are constraints. Information is therefore derived — compressed distinction — not fundamental. Starting with information flow means starting one rung too high on the emergence ladder.</p>
    <p>v0.2 corrects this. The primitive object is a constraint structure. Everything else — distinction, information, causality, geometry, gauge freedom — emerges from it in a specific order. This is not a cosmetic change. It changes what G is, how E8 fits, and which claims can be made.</p>
    <p>The black hole expansion mechanism is formally frozen in this version. It may be correct. It is not yet compiled at the level the lower ladder requires.</p>

    <h3>What Was Retained from v0.1</h3>
    <p>Three core intuitions survive the revision:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Spacetime geometry is not fundamental — it emerges from something deeper</li>
      <li>The Standard Model forces are not independently fundamental — they are faces of a single ambient structure</li>
      <li>Black holes play a non-trivial role in the geometry of the universe beyond simply absorbing matter</li>
    </ol>
    <p>What changed is the mathematical foundation beneath those intuitions.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section id="primitive">
    <h2>2 · The Primitive Object: Constraint Structure</h2>

    <h3>2.1 · Motivation</h3>
    <p>The v0.1 paper took information flow as given. Adversarial review identified the problem: information already assumes structure. You cannot define information without distinguishable states, and you cannot have distinguishable states without something that makes them different from each other. That something is prior to information. It is constraint.</p>
    <p>The question becomes: what is the smallest mathematical object that can generate distinction, information, causality, geometry, and gauge freedom without smuggling any of them in as assumptions?</p>

    <h3>2.2 · Definition</h3>

    <div class="conjecture">
      <span class="conjecture-label">Definition — Constraint System</span>
      <p>A constraint system is a pair K = (X, A) where X is the space of possible configurations and A ⊆ X × X is the set of admissible transitions. No geometry is assumed. No information is assumed. No metric, no measure, no topology beyond what is induced by A. Only the distinction between allowed and forbidden.</p>
    </div>

    <p>This is the primitive object of GUES v0.2.</p>

    <h3>2.3 · The Emergence Ladder</h3>
    <p>From K alone, the following structure emerges:</p>

    <h4>Distinction</h4>
    <p>Two configurations x₁, x₂ ∈ X are distinguishable if and only if there exists y ∈ X such that (x₁, y) ∈ A but (x₂, y) ∉ A, or vice versa. Distinction is difference in constraint profile — not an intrinsic label, but a relational property that emerges from the structure of A.</p>

    <h4>Information</h4>
    <p>Once distinction exists, information becomes I(x) = −log μ([x]) where [x] is the equivalence class of configurations sharing identical constraint profiles. Information is compressed distinction — not primitive.</p>

    <div class="equation">I(x) = −log μ([x])</div>

    <h4>Causality</h4>
    <p>A causal relation emerges when admissible transitions form a partial order: x ≺ y if and only if (x → y) ∈ A and no cycles exist. Causality is constraint-directed reachability — not a fundamental feature of spacetime.</p>

    <h4>Geometry</h4>
    <p>Geometry emerges when the graph induced by A admits a continuum limit. Distance becomes the minimal admissible path length. The smooth Lorentzian manifold of General Relativity is a continuum approximation of this induced structure at classical scales.</p>

    <h4>Gauge Freedom</h4>
    <p>Gauge freedom emerges when multiple internal descriptions produce identical constraint observables. The gauge symmetry group becomes:</p>

    <div class="equation">G = Aut(K)</div>

    <p>the automorphisms preserving constraint structure. Gauge symmetry is a redundancy generated by deeper consistency — not a fundamental symmetry imposed on the theory.</p>

    <div class="ladder">
      Constraint → Distinction → Information → Causality → Geometry → Gauge Freedom
    </div>

    <p>Each rung is derived from the one below. No rung is assumed.</p>

    <h3>2.4 · The Revised Central Conjecture</h3>

    <div class="conjecture">
      <span class="conjecture-label">Conjecture 1 — Constraint-First Emergence</span>
      <p>Spacetime geometry, information, and gauge symmetry are not independently fundamental. They are successive emergent properties of a primitive constraint system K = (X, A). The observable universe — its geometry, its forces, its causal structure — is a derived description of the admissibility structure of the most fundamental constraint system accessible to physical inquiry.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section id="forces">
    <h2>3 · Forces as Constraint Automorphisms</h2>

    <h3>3.1 · The Standard Model Gauge Groups Revisited</h3>
    <p>In the constraint-first framework, the Standard Model gauge groups are not cross-sections of a geometric object in any conventional sense. They are automorphism groups of different constraint structures — different admissibility regimes — within a single overarching K.</p>

    <div class="conjecture">
      <span class="conjecture-label">Conjecture 2 — Gauge Groups as Constraint Automorphisms</span>
      <p>The gauge groups U(1), SU(2), and SU(3) are the automorphism groups of constraint sub-structures within K. They are the residual symmetries of the admissibility structure after coarse-graining to different energy scales. The ambient object G is defined as Aut(K) — the full automorphism group of the primitive constraint system.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>3.2 · The Coleman-Mandula Problem</h3>
    <p>Adversarial review correctly identified the Coleman-Mandula theorem as a potential barrier: in any local relativistic quantum field theory, spacetime symmetries and internal gauge symmetries cannot be non-trivially mixed in a Lie group without killing all particle scattering.</p>
    <p>The constraint-first framework addresses this directly. G = Aut(K) is not a Lie group in the conventional sense. Spacetime symmetries and gauge symmetries emerge as distinct phases of the constraint structure — not as sub-algebras of a common Lie algebra. Coleman-Mandula applies to Lie algebras over smooth manifolds. The constraint system K generates the manifold; it does not presuppose it.</p>

    <div class="speculative">Whether the automorphism group of a constraint system of the required complexity is a well-defined mathematical object with the right properties to reproduce GR and the Standard Model at classical scales is the central open mathematical question of this conjecture.</div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section id="e8">
    <h2>4 · E8 as Fixed Point, Not Source</h2>

    <h3>4.1 · The Inversion</h3>
    <p>v0.1 treated E8 as a candidate ancestor of the Standard Model gauge groups. Adversarial review identified why this is dangerous: E8 appears in almost every unification attempt, and its appearance is therefore not discriminating evidence for any particular theory. More precisely, Lisi's E8 program failed because chiral fermions cannot be embedded in E8 without producing unwanted particles. Any approach treating E8 as a source inherits this problem.</p>

    <h3>4.2 · E8 as Attractor</h3>

    <div class="conjecture">
      <span class="conjecture-label">Conjecture 3 — E8 as Constraint Fixed Point</span>
      <p>E8 is not the source of Standard Model physics. It is a highly symmetric fixed point — an attractor — in the space of admissible constraint algebras under coarse-graining flows. There exists a coarse-graining flow F over constraint algebras such that E8 is a fixed point F(E8) = E8, or an attractor such that repeated application of F to a broad basin of microscopic constraint systems converges to E8.</p>
    </div>

    <p>This changes the question from <em>how do we embed physics into E8</em> to <em>why does E8 keep appearing as a fixed point regardless of microscopic implementation?</em> The second question is a universality question — significantly harder to fake and significantly more powerful if answered.</p>

    <h3>4.3 · The Chirality Resolution Path</h3>
    <p>If E8 is a fixed point rather than a source, fermion statistics are not forced into E8 by geometric constraint. They emerge from the breaking pattern — from how the constraint system flows away from the E8 fixed point as it acquires structure at lower energy scales. Chirality is a property of the breaking, not of the unbroken symmetry.</p>

    <div class="speculative">This is a proposed resolution path, not a proof. Demonstrating that the observed chiral spectrum follows from the breaking pattern of the E8 fixed point under constraint-system coarse-graining is the specific mathematical work required to close this gap.</div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section id="vacuum">
    <h2>5 · The Shape of Nothing: Vacuum Symmetry</h2>

    <h3>5.1 · The Complement Principle</h3>
    <p>Instead of asking for the shape of existence — which is unbounded — ask for the shape of nothing. The symmetry group of the zero state is the most constrained object in physics and may be the most direct path to G.</p>
    <p>In the constraint-first framework: the zero state is the constraint system K₀ in which all transitions are trivially admissible or forbidden — the trivial constraint system. G is the automorphism group of K₀.</p>

    <div class="conjecture">
      <span class="conjecture-label">Conjecture 4 — Vacuum as Primitive Constraint State</span>
      <p>The quantum vacuum is the physical realization of the trivial constraint system K₀. Its symmetry group — the set of transformations leaving the admissibility structure of K₀ invariant — is the ambient object G. The Standard Model gauge groups are the residual automorphisms after K₀ acquires non-trivial admissibility structure. The shape of nothing is the shape of G. Physical reality is the geometry of G's breaking.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>5.2 · Connection to Spontaneous Symmetry Breaking</h3>
    <p>The Higgs mechanism is already a story about the vacuum acquiring structure and breaking a symmetry. What GUES proposes is that the full Standard Model — not just electroweak unification — is the story of the vacuum's symmetry breaking from G all the way down.</p>
    <p>The difference from standard GUT approaches is the entry point. GUTs search for a larger Lie group. GUES searches for the automorphism group of the primitive constraint system — which is not assumed to be a Lie group.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section id="frozen">
    <h2>6 · Frozen Section: Black Holes and Cosmic Expansion</h2>

    <div class="frozen">The black hole expansion mechanism from GUES v0.1 is formally frozen in this version. The claim that black holes function as information-to-geometry transducers whose cumulative output drives cosmic expansion may be correct. It is not compiled at the level the constraint-first framework requires.</div>

    <p>In the constraint-first framework, black holes are more precisely described as constraint reorganizers — regions where the admissibility structure of K undergoes extreme compression and reorganization. What they emit beyond Hawking radiation must be described in terms of constraint structure, not information directly.</p>

    <p>The section will unfreeze when the following compile:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>What does a black hole do to the constraint system K in its vicinity?</li>
      <li>Is the output describable as a modification to the admissibility structure of surrounding spacetime?</li>
      <li>If yes, does the cumulative effect produce a pressure term distinguishable from Λ?</li>
    </ol>

    <p>The cosmological homogeneity problem remains open. Dark energy is remarkably uniform. Black holes are not. Any mechanism linking localized constraint reorganization to uniform cosmic acceleration requires an explicit bridge that does not currently exist.</p>
  </section>

